========================================
CSS POSITIONING (Complete Notes)
========================================

Definition:
-----------
Positioning in CSS defines how an element is
placed in the webpage layout and how it behaves
relative to the normal document flow, parent
elements, or the viewport.

There are five types of positioning:

1. static
2. relative
3. absolute
4. fixed
5. sticky

========================================
POSITION: STATIC
========================================

Definition:
-----------
position: static is the default positioning
value of all HTML elements.

----------------------------------------

Key Characteristics:
--------------------
1. Follows normal document flow.
2. Occupies its natural space.
3. Offset properties (top, right, bottom, left)
   do NOT work.
4. Cannot be repositioned using offsets.

----------------------------------------

How It Works:
-------------
Elements appear in the order they are written.

Block elements:
- Take full width
- Stack vertically

Inline elements:
- Take required width
- Sit horizontally

----------------------------------------

Interview One-Line Answer:
---------------------------
position: static is the default value that
follows the normal document flow and does
not respond to offset properties.

========================================
POSITION: RELATIVE
========================================

Definition:
-----------
position: relative positions an element
relative to its original position in
the normal document flow.

----------------------------------------

Key Characteristics:
--------------------
1. Remains in normal flow.
2. Keeps its original space.
3. Can be moved using top, right, bottom, left.
4. Creates a positioning context for
   absolutely positioned children.

----------------------------------------

How It Works:
-------------
The element is first placed normally.
Then movement happens from that position.

Example:

    position: relative;
    top: 20px;
    left: 10px;

This moves the element:
- 20px downward
- 10px to the right

Other elements do NOT occupy its space.

----------------------------------------

Interview One-Line Answer:
---------------------------
position: relative moves an element from
its original position while keeping its
space in the normal document flow.

========================================
POSITION: ABSOLUTE
========================================

Definition:
-----------
position: absolute removes the element
from the normal document flow and positions
it relative to the nearest positioned ancestor.

----------------------------------------

Key Characteristics:
--------------------
1. Removed from normal flow.
2. Does NOT keep its original space.
3. Other elements behave as if it does not exist.
4. Positioned relative to the nearest ancestor
   with position other than static.
5. If no positioned ancestor exists,
   it positions relative to the
   Initial Containing Block.

----------------------------------------

How It Works:
-------------
Example:

    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    right: 0;

The element will attach to the top-right
corner of its nearest positioned parent.

If no such parent exists,
it attaches to the Initial Containing Block.

----------------------------------------

Difference from Fixed:
-----------------------
- Absolute scrolls with the page.
- Fixed does NOT scroll.

----------------------------------------

Interview One-Line Answer:
---------------------------
position: absolute removes the element
from normal flow and positions it relative
to the nearest positioned ancestor.

========================================
POSITION: FIXED
========================================

Definition:
-----------
position: fixed positions an element relative
to the viewport (browser window).

The element remains fixed in the same position
even when the page is scrolled.

----------------------------------------

Key Characteristics:
--------------------
1. Removed from normal document flow.
2. Does not occupy space in the layout.
3. Positioned relative to the viewport.
4. Does NOT move when scrolling.
5. May overlap other elements.

----------------------------------------

How It Works:
-------------
Example:

    position: fixed;
    top: 10px;
    right: 20px;

This places the element:
- 10px from the top of the viewport
- 20px from the right of the viewport

----------------------------------------

Interview One-Line Answer:
---------------------------
position: fixed positions an element
relative to the viewport and keeps it
fixed even when scrolling.
